<%# The arrival curtain: two banks of cloud that part sideways to reveal the hall.
    Rendered only when the theme has intro on (db/pageThemesRepo.js's `intro`).

    Deliberately CSS-driven rather than script-driven. The overlay is opaque from the very first
    byte of markup - a deferred script could not cover the page before it paints - and its own
    animation is what clears it, so a browser with JavaScript off, or a failed script load, still
    gets a page it can use. public/js/throne-intro.js only ADDS things: cutting the animation
    short on the first user action, and replaying it when the page comes back out of the
    back/forward cache.

    Both halves carry a CSS fog gradient AND a photograph. The gradient paints immediately while
    the photo is still arriving over the network, which matters here more than usual: this
    overlay blocks the page for as long as it runs, so it must never be waiting on a download. %>
<div class="throne-intro" id="throne-intro" role="presentation" aria-hidden="true">
  <%# The seam comes first so it paints UNDER the two banks - it fills the strip where their
      feathered inner edges overlap and neither is fully opaque on its own. %>
  <div class="throne-intro__seam"></div>
  <div class="throne-intro__bank throne-intro__bank--left"></div>
  <div class="throne-intro__bank throne-intro__bank--right"></div>
</div>
